====Noun====
{{en-noun|es}}

# {{lb|en|India}} A [[groom]], or servant with responsibility for the horses.
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Thomas Williamson|title=Oriental Field Sports; being a Complete, Detailed, and Accurate Description of the Wild Sports of the East [...] In Two Volumes|location=London|publisher=Printed by [[w:William Bulmer (printer)|W[illiam] Bulmer and Co.]] Cleveland-Row, [[w:St James's|St. James's]]; for Edward Orme, [[w:Bond Street|Bond-Street]], the corner of [[w:Brook Street|Brook-Street]], and B. Crosby and Co. Stationers' Court|year=1808|volume=II|page=213|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=1BlcAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA213|oclc=225413285|passage=Not one of them [horse dealers] will venture a horse, he is about to sell, in the stables of the intended purchaser, unless attended by one of his own '''''syces''''', or grooms, who both knows and is known by the animal. If the horse be very old, or naturally dull, the '''syce''' takes care to ply him with spices and other stimulants; and if vicious, opium, and other anodynes are given; so that the horse is absolutely in a state of disguise.}}
#* {{quote-journal|lang=en|title=General Return of Casualties in the Army of the Punjaub in the Action at Goojerat on the 21st February 1849, between the British Forces under the Personal Command of General the Right Hon. [[w:Hugh Gough, 1st Viscount Gough|Hugh Baron Gough]], G.C.B., [[w:Commander-in-Chief, India|Commander-in-Chief of the Forces in India]], and the Rebel Army under the Sirdar Chuttur Singh and Rajah Shere Singh|journal=The Indian News, and Chronicle of Eastern Affairs|city=London|date=19 April 1849|issue=160|page=179|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=KHhNAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA179|oclc=64234884|passage=4th Troop 1st Brigade—3 rank and file, 1 '''syce''', 17 horses, killed; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Kipling Plain Tales|chapter=Miss Youghal's Sais|page=30|passage=Here all trace of him was lost, until a '''''sais''''' or groom met me on the Simla Mall with this extraordinary note:— {{...}}}}
#* '''1890''', [[w:Flora Annie Steel|Flora Annie Webster Steel]]; Grace Gardiner, “The Duties of the Servants”, in ''The Complete Indian Housekeeper and Cook: Giving the Duties of Mistress and Servants, the General Management of the House and Practical Recipes for Cooking in all its Branches'', Edinburgh: F. Murray, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/228145908 228145908]</small>; reprinted Cambridge: {{w|Cambridge University Press}}, 2010, <small>[[Special:BookSources/9781108021937|978-1-108-02193-7]]</small>, [https://books.google.com/books?id=HgzjIUJndx8C&pg=PA68 page 68]:
#*: THE DUTIES OF THE '''''SAIS''''' OR GROOM. {{...}} Now, if the good house-mother's proudest boast is that not even "the cattle within her gate" fail to feel her kindly care, she will often find it necessary to take an active part in teaching the '''''sais''''' his duty, and seeing that the horses receive proper attention. {{...}} The old plan of a '''''sais''''' and a grass-cutter to each horse is a thing of the past, and the number of '''''saises''''' or grooms should have reference merely to the amount of harnessing and out-work necessary during the day.
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Charles A[tmore] Sherring|chapter=Superstitions|title=Western Tibet and the British Borderland; the Sacred Country of Hindus and Buddhists, with an Account of the Government, Religion, and Customs of its Peoples|location=London|publisher=[[w:Edward Arnold (publisher)|Edward Arnold]]|year=1906|page=100|oclc=3827957|passage=But apart from the story of the havildar, my own '''syce''', a hillman who attends my pony, has actually seen two ghosts, with one of whom he held a long conversation.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=E[dward] M[organ] Forster|authorlink=E. M. Forster|chapter=|title={{w|A Passage to India}}|location=London|publisher=[[w:Edward Arnold (publisher)|Edward Arnold & Co.]]|year=1924|oclc=558998361|location2=New York, N.Y.|publisher2=[[w:Harcourt (publisher)|Harcourt, Brace & World]]|year2=1952|page2=13|pageurl2=https://archive.org/stream/passagetoindiafors00fors#page/13/mode/1up/|oclc2=11177613|passage=Dinner, dinner, dinner&nbsp;… but when he returned to the house for it, Mahmoud Ali had drifted away in his turn, to speak to his '''sais'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Apa Pant|chapter=Dreams and Destinations|title=A Moment in Time|location=Bombay|publisher=[[w:Orient Blackswan|Orient Longman]]|year=1974|page=76|isbn=837947596|passage=The horse also, as if it had its own premonitions, refused to get on to those rickety planks, though the rest of the party had negotiated them safely. The '''''sais''''' (horse attendant), who had no such anxieties, crossed ahead of us and struggled for some minutes to drag the poor frightened animal, with myself on its back, across the chasm.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Sara Banerji|authorlink=Sara Banerji|title=The Wedding of Jayanthi Mandel|location=London|publisher={{w|Victor Gollancz Ltd}}.|year=1987|isbn=978-0-575-03984-1|passage=We have not been able to get a good '''syce''' for our animal, and have had to make do with a young and inexperienced fellow.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Rudyard Kipling|authorlink=Rudyard Kipling|coauthors=James Daley, comp.|chapter=The Maltese Cat|title=Great Horse Stories|location=Mineola, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Dover Publications}}|year=2010|page=95|isbn=978-0-486-47669-8|passage=Each pony had his '''sais''', his groom, who lived and ate and slept with the animal, and had betted a good deal more than he could afford on the result of the game. There was no chance of anything going wrong, but to make sure, each '''sais''' was shampooing the legs of his pony to the last minute. Behind the '''saises''' sat as many of the Skidars' regiment as had leave to attend the match {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|[[Malaya]]|dated}} ''usually'' '''[[syce]]''': [[chauffeur]], [[driver]].[[File:COLLECTIE TROPENMUSEUM Auto met M.C. Westerman het echtpaar Soesters en de chauffeur TMnr 60026697.jpg|thumb|A 1919 photograph of M. C. Westerman, Mr. and Mrs. Soeters, and a chauffeur, in {{w|Ambarawa}}, {{w|Central Java}}, {{w|Dutch East Indies}}]]
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Ralph Lionel German|title=Handbook to British Malaya, 1935|location=[London]|publisher=[R. L. German]: Obtainable from the Malay Information Agency, {{w|Waterlow and Sons}}|year=1935|page=50|oclc=27325434|passage=House servants are usually either Chinese or Tamil, the former predominating, especially in towns of any size. The domestic staff will in general consist of a houseboy (in large establishments two houseboys), a water carrier (''tukang ayer''), whose duties include washing dishes and preparing baths, a cook, a gardener, a chauffeur or '''''sais''''', and perhaps an ''ayah'' (if Chinese, ''amah'') or two, according to the size of the family.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Margaret Pemberton|authorlink=Margaret Pemberton|title=A Multitude of Sins|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Bantam Books}}|year=1988|oclc=746914751|passage=[A]fter their first few days there he had hired a '''''syce''''', a Malay chauffeur. The '''''syce''''', who had been squatting down, sheltering in the shade of the car, jumped to his feet at their approach, opening the doors for them with an efficient flourish.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Laurence C[arl] Bergquist|title=Destiny: A Southeast Asia Saga, 1928–1953: Singapore, Malaya, Indonesia|location=Pacifica, Calif.|publisher=Pacifica Press|year=1994|page=51|isbn=978-0-935553-06-2|passage=Each establishment had a retinue of Chinese or Malay servants, on average consisting of a cook, one or two "house boys," a ''tukang kebun'' (gardener), a '''''sais''''' (chauffeur), and an ''ayah'' or ''amah'' (maid or nurse) to take care of ironing {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Mary Sydney Burke|title=The Soldier's Wife: Peace and War|location=London|publisher=Janus Publishing Company|year=1999|page=128|isbn=978-1-85756-490-7|passage=The lady – a very elegant Romanian by birth – arrived with her daughter Miriam, the English governess, the secretary, a detective, and last of all the '''''syce''''', or chauffeur, who seated himself in the garden rather than wait in the large Rolls-Royce parked outside, with the family crest placed above the number plate.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Christopher [Alan] Bayly|authorlink=Christopher Bayly|coauthors=Tim[othy Norman] Harper|title=Forgotten Armies: The Fall of British Asia, 1941–1945|location=Cambridge, Mass.|publisher=[[w:Harvard University Press|The Belknap Press of Harvard University Press]]|year=2005|page=53|isbn=978-0-674-01748-1|passage=[T]he expansion of the service economy, for the home, the office and the municipality, had created a more mixed labouring world. A wealthy European or Asian home would bring together a Chinese ''amah'', or maid, a Malay '''''syce''''', or chauffeur, and an Indian ''kebun'', or gardener, operating through a Malay lingua franca. On a larger scale, in the invisible city, ethnic communities were pushed closer together, often for the first time.}}

