#* {{quote-journal|lang=en|title=Editor's Drawer|magazine=[[w:Harper's Magazine|Harper's New Monthly Magazine]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Harper (publisher)|Harper & Brothers, Publishers]], 329 & 331 [[w:Pearl Street (Manhattan)|Pearl Street]], [[w:Franklin Square (Manhattan)|Franklin Square]]|month=August|year=1855|volume=XI|issue=LXIII|page=425|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=C3cCAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA425|oclc=1641392|passage='Yes,' '''sais''' I, 'what's left of me; but, good gracious,' '''sais''' I, 'you have got the 'heaves.' I hope it ain't catchin'.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Frederic Remington|authorlink=Frederic Remington|chapter=Sun-Down Leflare's Warm Spot|editor=John D. Seelye|title=Stories of the Old West: Tales of the Mining Camp, Cavalry Troop, & Cattle Ranch|location=Norman, Ok.|publisher={{w|University of Oklahoma Press}}|year=2000|page=203|isbn=978-0-8061-3283-9|passage=Well, he tak some young man un he go off to Alsaroke to steal horse, un I seet roun' un watch dat woman. She watch me. Pretty soon camp was hunt de buffalo, un I was hunt Snow-Owl's woman. Every one was excite, un dey don' tak no 'count of me. I see de woman go up leetle coulie for stray horse, un I follar her. I '''sais''', 'How do? You come be my woman. We run off to Meestar MacDonnail's tradehouse.' {{...}} She '''sais''' she afraid.}}

